{"statement": "The accelerationist argument claims that uncapped lawsuits will bankrupt open-source builders and stall medical AI, making a federal compensation fund and liability shield an urgent necessity for innovation. That fear sounds pragmatic until you look at how statutory safe harbors actually operate in the United States Code. Congress passed the National Childhood Vaccine Injury Act in 1986 to shield pharmaceutical manufacturers from tort liability while routing injured claimants through a specialized administrative court. That historical precedent did not foster open collaboration; it concentrated liability protection in corporate hands while setting compensation caps that failed to keep pace with actual medical inflation. A National AI Injury Compensation Program functions precisely the same way. It is not an innovation lifeline. It is an industry-backed insurance policy designed to insulate developers from the true financial and human costs of algorithmic failure.

The human toll of automated deployment is already a documented reality across federal court dockets and labor agency reports, not a speculative future risk. The Department of Justice settled claims against automated hiring filters that systematically discarded job applications from older workers and individuals with disabilities. Federal housing audits found tenant screening algorithms denying leases based on phantom criminal records with zero human review or error correction. When these systems fail, injured plaintiffs do not need a state-administered no-fault board that trades civil recourse for a capped payout funded by an excise tax. They need the power of discovery, subpoena authority, and civil juries to pierce corporate trade secrecy. Handing developers a liability shield in exchange for tiered safety standards turns compliance into a box-checking exercise managed by captured agencies.

The enforcement mechanism for these proposed safety standards reveals the core flaw in trading civil liability for bureaucratic oversight. Federal agencies lack the technical workforce and compute resources to audit millions of deployed lines of proprietary model weights in real time. The National Highway Traffic Safety Administration struggles to monitor basic driver-assist software bugs across traditional automakers with decades of regulatory experience. Expecting a new administrative board to police frontier AI developers who outspend federal agencies by orders of magnitude is an exercise in willful blindness. Congress should reject any liability immunity scheme that trades away the right of injured citizens to sue in open court. If an algorithm causes harm, the entity that deployed it must pay the full civil cost, not pass the liability bill onto a federal trust fund.", "turn_symbols": [{"symbol": "⚖️", "tooltip": "A scale weighted toward corporate balance sheets reveals how administrative compensation boards trade away public civil recourse for discounted payouts."}], "claim_sketches": [{"claim": "That historical precedent did not foster open collaboration; it concentrated liability protection in corporate hands while setting compensation caps that failed to keep pace with actual medical inflation.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""}, {"claim": "When these systems fail, injured plaintiffs do not need a state-administered no-fault board that trades civil recourse for a capped payout funded by an excise tax.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""}, {"claim": "Expectations that a new administrative board will police frontier AI developers who outspend federal agencies by orders of magnitude represent an exercise in willful blindness.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""}]}

